Zero 0.11 local prepared-release updater

The delivered programs are original Zero compiled directly to native Linux
x64 ELF and Windows x64 PE. They do not launch a shell, Python, PowerShell,
CMD, an external hash utility or a foreign language runtime. build.py and the
test scripts are external development/verification orchestration only.
The updater manages a separate local store and preserves installer manifest v1,
its base image and storage file. It does not modify a running or booted OS.

Native commands:
  zero-update init SOURCE.iso VERSION NEW_DIRECTORY
  zero-update stage SOURCE.iso VERSION DIRECTORY
  zero-update publish SOURCE.iso VERSION NEW_INBOX
  zero-update check INBOX DIRECTORY
  zero-update status DIRECTORY
  zero-update verify DIRECTORY
  zero-update rollback DIRECTORY
  zero-update inspect DIRECTORY
  zero-update recover SOURCE_DIRECTORY NEW_DIRECTORY
  zero-update watch INBOX DIRECTORY INTERVAL_SECONDS [CHECK_COUNT]
  zero-sha256 FILE
Windows uses zero-update.exe / zero-sha256.exe with the same arguments.

VERSION is a decimal release counter in 1..4294967295, not a parsed semantic
version string. The publisher chooses it; it is not inferred from ISO contents.
init creates a dedicated new directory and prepares its first image. stage
requires a strictly higher counter than any previously staged counter, even
after rollback. The ISO is copied to an exclusive temporary file, checked with
our streaming SHA256, flushed through the native file API, reopened and
independently hashed again, then atomically
published without replacing an existing path. Previous images are retained.
All selected/previous images must verify before any new selection is recorded.

publish creates a new local inbox containing system.iso and release.meta.
check validates that metadata and, if its counter is newer, automatically
prepares the inbox image and records the new PREPARED selection. A repeated
check is idempotent. A launcher can call check at startup; this program does
not register a daemon, scheduler or OS startup entry. The optional watch
command supplies a foreground native polling loop, described below; it does
not fetch from the network. An absent/unreadable inbox is an error,
not a claim that a remote server has no update. Metadata is always validated;
a corrupted newer inbox image is rejected. An inbox counter at or below the
stored high-water mark is a no-op, so its unused image is not read or hashed.

Example, using trusted local release media:
  zero-update init zero.iso 900 NEW_STORE
  zero-update publish next-zero.iso 901 NEW_INBOX
  zero-update check NEW_INBOX NEW_STORE
  zero-update status NEW_STORE
  zero-update rollback NEW_STORE
Every successful selection explicitly says PREPARED and NOT IMPLEMENTED for
boot activation. rollback changes only the prepared selection and preserves
the highest counter. Repeated rollback toggles the two most recent selections.
Older historical ISO files remain untouched; no garbage collection is provided.

The store can be a new child directory of a Zero installer preparation.
It never changes installer/system.iso, storage.data or zero.install. Installer
v1 removal preserves unknown updater directories. No partition table, boot
sector, BCD, GRUB, firmware entry or user data disk is modified. The optical
image format checker accepts the current Zero BIOS/UEFI catalog layout and a
consistent ZERO_SYSTEM ISO9660 envelope, including in-file catalog boot-image
extents; it does not prove a new image boots.
Images are limited to 1GiB. Actual boot testing remains a separate release gate.

The supplied native file API rejects nonregular files and final symlinks or
Windows reparse points. It does not secure linked ancestor directories or
provide a sandbox against another process changing the directory. Use a
private owned directory. An exclusive .update-lock prevents cooperating updater
instances from mutating the store simultaneously. Status/verify read without
creating a lock and fail closed if concurrent/incomplete state is observed.
Existing temporary, lock, version, metadata and destination files are never
silently overwritten or deleted. Normal copy failures delete only the current
invocation's exclusive temporary payload. Unrelated contents are preserved.

Journal publication is append-only. A successful append is flushed through
fsync on Linux or FlushFileBuffers on Windows, closed, reopened and byte-verified
before reporting success. A failed payload flush prevents image publication;
a failed journal flush reports failure and retains its lock and evidence. Each fixed record has a format,
sequence, transition invariants, commit marker and FNV-1a64 corruption check;
image digests are SHA256. A torn journal tail is an error. A failed partial
append retains .update-lock and all ISO files for inspection. Automatic journal
truncation or stale-lock deletion is deliberately absent. Explicit inspection
and recovery export a checked snapshot into a new directory, as below; they
never remove the original lock or rewrite the original journal. A crash between payload publication and the
journal can leave an unselected ISO; it is preserved and blocks reuse of that
filename. Maximum journal length is 4096 records; reaching it fails safely.
The shared API now flushes file contents, but does not synchronize parent
directories or guarantee durable rename ordering across power loss. Full
power-loss transaction durability and automatic crash recovery are not claimed.

SHA256 detects byte corruption; it does not authenticate a publisher. Neither
release.meta nor zero.updates is digitally signed. There is no configured
release server, TLS transport, trusted signing key, public URL or remote update
service. Do not treat an untrusted inbox as an authenticated update. Booting
an ISO inside a host filesystem and activating an updated boot slot are still
unimplemented. This is a real native local preparation/verification/rollback
backend, not a complete installed rolling-release operating system.

Source layout:
  declarations.zero.part     shared native variables/memory
  sha256.zero.part           original streaming SHA256/hash-and-copy worker
  main.zero.part             updater commands, journal and filesystem logic
  recovery.zero.part         exact embedded snapshot-recovery block
  hash-main.zero.part        standalone SHA256 CLI
  update.zero / sha256.zero  exact combined standalone Zero source files
  FORMAT.txt                persistent data contract
  verification/*.json        actual test results and tested hashes

Build with the repository's native host API compiler binaries:
  python3 updater/build.py
A candidate own compiler can be selected with --compiler PATH. build.json
records the exact compiler and output hashes.
This combines authored Zero fragments without translating their logic, then
runs the own compiler. The resulting binaries need no build script at runtime.
Linux verification: python3 updater/test-native.py
Windows PE verification under the existing Wine: python3 updater/test-windows.py
Wine evidence does not establish native Windows reparse or crash behavior.

Inspection and explicit recovery
  zero-update inspect DAMAGED_STORE
  zero-update recover DAMAGED_STORE NEW_RECOVERED_STORE

inspect is read-only. It validates every complete 256-byte journal record and
transition, the full selected/previous payload hashes, and a second byte-exact
read of the source journal. It reports an incomplete final tail in hexadecimal
bytes (00..ff). A whole invalid record, no complete initial record, or a missing
or changed referenced image is an error. Ordinary status/verify continue to
reject any partial tail; inspect does not pretend the damaged store is usable.

recover performs the same validation, creates a new exclusive directory and
copies the selected and previous payloads with flush plus independent SHA256
readback. It preserves every complete validated journal record and the highest
version counter. Only the explicitly reported incomplete final bytes are
excluded from the new journal. A complete latest record is retained even if
its source lock remains; recovery never silently rolls it back. Before new
journal publication the original journal is read again and must still match
the captured snapshot, including its partial tail. The copied journal is
flushed and byte-verified before non-overwriting publication.

Source lock, partial tail, historical images and unrelated source contents
are always untouched. A source lock is not assumed stale: inspection and
recovery do not mutate its directory. The result is a validated point-in-time
snapshot, not a lock against source changes immediately after verification.
Historical images outside the final selected/previous pair remain only in the
source; all their complete selection records remain in the recovered journal.
No recovery destination is reused. On failure, the new directory's lock,
partial copy and any completed files remain for inspection; no recovery
selection is published until all checks pass. A successful recovery removes
only its own new lock. Source must be a private owned directory; ancestor-link
and concurrent adversary sandbox guarantees are not provided.

Native local automatic checks
  zero-update watch TRUSTED_INBOX STORE 60
  zero-update watch TRUSTED_INBOX STORE 1 2

The first command keeps checking every 60 seconds after the preceding check
finishes, until interrupted or any error occurs. The second performs exactly
two checks with a one-second wait between them, then exits. Intervals are
1..86400 seconds and optional counts 1..100000. The wait uses native nanosleep
or Sleep through Zero's hostwait primitive; it neither busy-spins nor launches
another program. Each cycle performs the same verification, exclusive locking
and local newer-image preparation as check. No lock is held while waiting.
An absent/unreadable inbox, invalid metadata, corrupted newer image, lock
conflict or host wait failure
stops the process with an error; there is no misleading successful retry.
This is foreground polling of a trusted local directory, not a remote update
service, login/startup registration or automatic boot-slot activation.

Focused verification
  python3 updater/test-recovery.py
  python3 updater/test-recovery.py --wine
These cover partial/full-invalid journals, high-water preservation, source
preservation, exclusive destinations, partial copies, bounded native watch,
and missing/corrupt inboxes. Linux additionally injects payload/journal fsync
failures and a concurrent journal change through external strace. These are
error-path checks, not hardware power-loss durability or native Windows tests.
